    {
      "id": "1566433537588989984",
      "type": "Default",
      "timestamp": "2026-11-01T12:47:38.289+00:00",
      "timestampEdited": null,
      "callEndedTimestamp": null,
      "isPinned": false,
      "content": "Primary testing would be 4-wire measurements of nets through the bonds at both ends; where applicable isolation testing is also on the docket.\nWhere there are more than 2 pads for each net bonded out (there are 4 pads on the die for each net, but most DUT bonding sites on the test flex won't have all of them), the results from pairwise 4-wire measurements ought to reveal per-bond resistance, though possibly it might require adding a third voltage measurement wire into it, I'm not sure off the top of my head.",
      "author": {
        "id": "262706220073222144",
        "name": "namibj",
        "discriminator": "0000",
        "nickname": "namibj",
        "color": null,
        "isBot": false,
        "roles": [],
        "avatarUrl": "2026-11_media/f885abc0dddd0a33e4d4991b3c99091d-7ACD2.png"
      },
      "attachments": [],
      "embeds": [],
      "stickers": [],
      "reactions": [],
      "mentions": [],
      "inlineEmojis": []
    },
    {
      "id": "1566494216878563390",
      "type": "Reply",
      "timestamp": "2026-11-01T16:48:45.359+00:00",
      "timestampEdited": null,
      "callEndedTimestamp": null,
      "isPinned": false,
      "content": "yeah works.\n\nI took a look at the decoder generation code and it seems `RowPreDecodersT` could be taught to handle `(address_groups[0] != 3)`, specifically, `(address_groups[0] == 4)`, for a straight forward one extra bit (so up to 1024 words), as well as an easy extension by allowing to layer another pre-decoder in front of the current pre-decoder, where the new pre-decoder would merely act on this new (optional) 4th input bit of the current pre-decoder stage.\nIt'd have to spread them a bit more vertically I think to not get routing congestion at the current last level decoder/page pitch, but otherwise I think it should work? And like, without having to make the row periphery wider. Unless this would not possibly fit in the limited routing channels on the left side over the input DFFs; it does look quite tight there. On further look it does appear to be mighty tight already....\nSeems like it'd still be worth it though, at least with a word size notably larger than the tiny 8 bit (so that the extra decoder column of row periphery space doesn't dominate).\n\n@Tholin How large of an SRAM would you _want_ to stick in that corner if you would get to wish anything the FlexMem compiler could support if it had sufficient demand to warrant the implementation/support effort?",
      "author": {
        "id": "262706220073222144",
        "name": "namibj",
        "discriminator": "0000",
        "nickname": "namibj",
        "color": null,
        "isBot": false,
        "roles": [],
        "avatarUrl": "2026-11_media/f885abc0dddd0a33e4d4991b3c99091d-7ACD2.png"
